# simpl_api integration guide

> **Maintained by**: the `simpl_api` repo, auto-synced to simpl_knowledge.
> **Source of truth**: `simpl-techs/simpl_api/.agent/SKILL.md`

## What this service is

`simpl_api` is the FastAPI backend for the simpl sales intelligence platform. It exposes app/admin API routes, webhook handlers, guided/autopilot endpoints, prompt/template services, background jobs, and agent-facing backend workflows.

## Installation

```bash
conda env create -f environment.yml
conda activate simpl_api
poetry install
cp .env.example .env
```

This repo is normally deployed as a service, not installed as a dependency by other repos.

## Basic usage

```bash
conda activate simpl_api
poetry run uvicorn simpl_api.main:app --reload
```

When adding API behavior, place HTTP route code under `src/simpl_api/api`, reusable business logic in `simpl_core` or `src/simpl_api/services`, and schemas under `src/simpl_api/schemas`.

## Rules and conventions

- Keep FastAPI route handlers thin. Move reusable domain behavior to `simpl_core` or a service module.
- Use Pydantic schemas for request/response contracts.
- Keep environment variables documented in `README.md` and `.env.example` when adding new config.
- Use existing agent/service patterns before introducing a new orchestration style.
- Emails a user's SimpL agent sends go through the `email` channel plugin and `services/channels/agent_email.py`: a plain email in the agent's words, one link into SimpL, signed as the user's own agent persona. Pass `brain.notify_user` a `body` (and optionally `subject`, `route`); don't build HTML.
- A seller's outbound email signature is `GET`/`PUT /api/v1/app/email-signature` (`html`, `plain`). HTML is sanitized with `simpl_core.services.email.signature` and stored on `sales.user.email_signature_html`. Apply `migrations/20260907_user_email_signature.sql` before this route is deployed. The journey email flow appends it at send time; this route does not send mail.
- A seller's active hours, when the extension may send, are `outreach_prefs.active_hours` = `{"start": "HH:MM", "stop": "HH:MM", "timezone": "<IANA name>"}` (start inclusive, stop exclusive, start before stop). A missing field takes 07:00 / 19:00 / UTC, and an unset or invalid window runs on 07:00-19:00 UTC. `sales.resolve_active_hours(prefs jsonb[, at timestamptz])` resolves it: the extension task RPC gates on it, and `GET`/`PATCH /api/v1/app/preferences` return its answer as `active_hours_effective` (`start`, `stop`, `timezone`, `source` = `configured` | `unset` | `invalid`, `is_open_now`). `PATCH` merges `active_hours` one level deep, `null` resets it, and a value the resolver calls invalid, or a zone not spelled exactly as in IANA, is refused with 422. Read the window through the resolver (`services/active_hours.py`) instead of re-parsing the JSON. Review-answer follow-ups (`review_response_service._next_business_morning`) schedule at the start of the seller's window, 09:00 Europe/Rome without one, like simpl_core's retry triggers (simpl_core >= 0.8.2).
- Authenticate product routes with `auth.get_current_user`, which also refuses SimpL Challenge entrants; scope them with `simpl_api.guards`, whose `resolve_user_context` repeats that refusal against the database. Both are single places, so a new endpoint written against the ordinary dependencies inherits them. Do not reimplement either check per endpoint.
- Approval routes (journey-log `set-pending`, managed approve, Agent API draft approve and `bulk-approve`) and invite execution return the status the database settled on. simpl_core's dispatch authority rule can send an approved row back to `PENDING_APPROVAL` (`payload.authority.block_reason`) or cancel it, so callers must not assume `PENDING`; `bulk-approve` lists such rows under `withheld`, and invite execution answers 409.
- Regenerating a pending draft is asynchronous (simpl_core `request_regenerate`; the outreach worker rewrites it). Seller: `POST /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/regenerate` `{additional_prompt?}` (other legacy fields are ignored) answers 202 `{kind: "queued", journey_log_id, request_id, trigger_id, requested_at, late_after_seconds: 900, regenerate_pending}`, where `regenerate_pending` is the draft's `payload.regenerate_pending` marker; only message drafts (`send_message`, `linkedin_connect`, `invite_call`) awaiting approval qualify (409 `invalid_transition`), 404 when the draft is not the caller's. Poll `GET /api/v1/app/journey-log/regenerate-jobs/{trigger_id}?request_id=` for `{status: queued|running|rewritten|replaced|review_request|no_result|failed|cancelled, trigger_id, request_id, journey_log_id, scope, requested_at, late, reason, reasoning, version_index, new_journey_log_id, new_journey_log_status}` (404 when not the caller's job). `POST /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/regenerate/cancel` answers `{cancelled, request_id, trigger_id}` once the regenerate failed or is late (15 minutes), else 409 `regenerate_not_cancellable`. Managed operators have the same three at `/api/v1/admin/managed/actions/{id}/regenerate`, `/api/v1/admin/managed/actions/regenerate-jobs/{trigger_id}` and `/{id}/regenerate/cancel`, acting for the draft's owner (`requested_by: admin:<id>`). The Agent API's `POST /api/agent/v1/drafts/{id}/regenerate` answers 202 `{journey_log_id, status: "queued", request_id, trigger_id, agent_instructions}` and `GET /api/agent/v1/drafts/regenerate-jobs/{trigger_id}` the same job shape. An approved Sales Domain `writer_regenerate` queues a forced rewrite per draft through simpl_core's command (`queued` / `skipped` / `failed` per row).
- While a regenerate is queued or running the draft is locked: every journey-log write (seller and Managed approve, edit, reject, reschedule, version switch, regenerate, review answers, invite execution, manual email/invite confirmation, Agent API approve/reject/update) answers 409 `{"detail": {"code": "regenerate_in_progress", "message": "..."}}` (the Agent API adds `error` and `agent_instructions`). Agent API `bulk-approve`/`bulk-reject` leave such drafts alone and list them under `regenerating`. A regenerate that failed unlocks the draft and keeps its marker (`state: failed`) for the card.
- Outreach previews are retired: `POST /api/v1/outreach/preview`, the `dry_run`/`preview` modes of `POST /api/v1/outreach/simulate` (`mode` is now required, `persist` or `traced`) and the customer chat's `build_outreach_preview` tool. simpl_api runs no outreach walk and no longer depends on simpl-outreach.
- A colleague's hand-off is passed on from one of its cards: `GET /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/handoff-redirect` returns the objective behind the card (`objective_id`, `responsibility_key`, `state`) and the colleagues it can go to (`targets[]`: `user_id`, `full_name`, `responsibility_key`, `description`). `POST` with `{to_user_id, reason?}` moves it (simpl_core `AccountCoordinationService.redirect`). The targets come from the team table, not from the caller. The old hand-off closes and its queued cards are cancelled, and the new owner is woken. A card that isn't the caller's, or has no `coordination_objective_id`, gets 404. A colleague the table does not allow gets 403, naming the allowed ones. A closed hand-off, or an open one already on the target's responsibility, gets 409. Repeating the same move replays it (`replayed: true`). Managed operators have the same pair at `/api/v1/admin/managed/actions/{id}/handoff-redirect`: the managed-scope check resolves the card's owner, the move is made as that owner, and the operator is recorded in `sales.audit_log` (`managed.action.handoff_redirect`).
- "Propose other times" on an invite card: `POST /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/propose-times` with `{times: [{start, timezone}] (1-3), note?}` (`start` is ISO 8601 with its UTC offset).
  - **Checks:** the times are held to simpl_core's `proposed_times_problems` (future, offset matching the IANA zone, no duplicates); otherwise 422 `{code: invalid_times, problems}`. A card that isn't the caller's gets 404. A card that isn't an `invite_call` gets 409 `not_an_invite`. A card not awaiting approval gets 409 `not_pending`. A closed hand-off gets 409 `handoff_closed`.
  - **Effect:** the card is cancelled (`payload.cancellation.reason = seller_proposed_other_times`, with the times and the note), in one transaction with one of two effects:
    - `route: own_conversation`, for a card with no hand-off: a `human_review_resolved` re-entry on the card's connection, with `selected_option_id = propose_other_times`, `proposed_times` and `note`. simpl-outreach replies offering exactly those times.
    - `route: handoff_source`, for a card linked to an open objective: the owner's `need_information` with `proposed_times`, which wakes the colleague who started the hand-off, since they are the one talking to the lead.
  - **Replays and events:** repeating the call on a card already cancelled this way replays it (`replayed: true`) and writes nothing. A `user.action.proposed_other_times` outreach event is logged.
  - **Managed:** operators have the same route at `/api/v1/admin/managed/actions/{id}/propose-times`. It acts as the card's owner, records the operator in `cancellation.cancelled_by` (`admin:<id>`), and writes `sales.audit_log` (`managed.action.propose_times`).

- The funnel's ROI block is `POST /api/v1/app/reports/value` and `POST .../value/seat-request`. It is a staff beta: only SimpL staff (`is_admin`) get it, 403 for everyone else, and the response says `beta: true`. Setting `ROI_RELEASED_TO_CUSTOMERS=true` (also `1`/`yes`/`on`) in the API environment releases it to the customer's owners, billing admins and ICP managers; unset or any other value keeps it staff-only. The cost follows the model Stripe bills (a pay-per-call account line means pay per call; the database model only when Stripe cannot say). Staff can send `simulate_pay_per_call: true` to price a seat customer as if it paid per call (`simulated: true`, `viewer.can_simulate`); anyone else gets 403. The seat request is how ICP managers ask owners and billing admins for accounts through the governed in-app notification. `rolling` (7, 30, 90) is the window of the funnel metrics beside the block and `offset` (0-104) moves it back that many windows; the response's `period` is that window itself, not a pace: its leads and meetings, the new customers its meetings bring at the close rate, their value, what SimpL cost in it (seats: the subscription for its days at today's price; pay per call: the calls charged in it) and `roi`, with `has_earlier` to stop going back at the first lead, or at the window pay per call began in (`cost_counted_from` set, `roi` empty: the seats paid before are unknown). The monthly figures and the forecast are that window's pace. All arithmetic lives in `services/customer_value/model.py`. `sales.icp.deal_size` means the yearly value of one new customer won from that ICP, in the customer's billing currency; it is set with `PATCH /api/v1/app/icps/{icp_id}/deal-size`, and the ROI prefers it over the median of `sales.deal`.
- Billing runs without rollout switches: the pay-per-call cycle (record, three-day contest window, Stripe posting, invoices), the hourly Growth price transitions and the call-evidence moves into Negotiation always run. What a customer pays is decided per customer, by its billing model and its Stripe subscription. An account that must pay nothing (SimpL's own) is a pay-per-call customer in billing mode `comp`, a 100% coupon on its subscription: its calls are recorded, posted and invoiced like anyone's, at zero. The call-evidence job applies the evidence of the last 30 days it has not applied yet, at most 500 per run.
- Pay per call (`sales.customer_subscription.billing_model = 'pay_per_call'`): accounts are free and each company is charged once, when its first call that took place is attributable to SimpL (`sales_view.billable_calls`: the company moved into Negotiation, or into Closed, backed by evidence that the call took place (Recall recording, calendar acceptance, the outreach agent reading the post-call conversation, the seller's or an admin's confirmation); the customer claimed it in SimpL and wrote to it before the meeting), from `billing_model_since` on, at its ICP's `sales.icp.call_price_cents`, EUR 70-150 in 10 EUR tiers from the pool size (`services/call_pricing.py`). A charge waits three days from the call, and at least three days from when it is recorded (`held`, `CONTEST_WINDOW`), in which any user of the customer can contest it (`POST /api/v1/app/calls/charges/{id}/contest`; `GET /api/v1/app/calls/charges?from&to` lists them by call date with their owner and totals, the amounts only for owners, billing admins and managers: `viewer.can_see_amounts`; `GET /api/v1/app/calls/billing-model` says whether the customer pays per call; `GET /calls/billing-status` carries each company's `charge_id` for the board), before `background_jobs/call_billing.py` posts it as a Stripe pending invoice item on the customer's subscription, which the monthly invoice collects. `sales.call_charge` is the only record of charged calls. The model follows the linked Stripe subscription (its `simpl_pay_per_call_account` line), set by `link_stripe_subscription` and payment-link completion. Admin routes: `/api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/call-charges` (list, void, credit, retry, manual add, `{charge_id}/resolve` for a contested one), `/admin/customers/{id}/call-pricing`, `/admin/icps/{id}/call-price`, `/admin/call-pricing/quote`, `/admin/call-pricing/icps`; payment links, `POST /admin/customers/{id}/subscription/create` and onboarding `billing_options` take `product = "pay_per_call"`. Stripe objects: `scripts/stripe_setup_pay_per_call.py`.
- The admin outreach case view reads (all `require_admin`, read-only): `GET /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/call-charges` takes an optional `company_id` and each charge also says `created_at`, `updated_at`, `contested_by` (+ `contested_by_name`), `resolved_by`, `resolved_at`, `ulc_id`, `history_id`, `claimed_at` (the Stripe posting claim) and `owner_user_id` / `owner_name` (the app's Calls tab shows customers exactly what it did); `GET /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/companies/{company_id}/meetings?past_days=365&future_days=90` (caps 730 / 180, 422 beyond) lists the customer's calendar meetings with the company's leads from Recall's synced calendars (simpl_core `LeadMeetings.for_lead`; the company's leads and the customer's connections at it, with an email address, at most 200: `leads_checked`, `truncated`), one row per `meeting_key` with its `lead_ids`, 404 for an unknown company, never written to `sales.meeting_booking`; `GET /api/v1/admin/conversations` gives every message `journey_log_id` (`provider_metadata.journey_log_id`, emails only today) and `provider_message_id`, null when absent. `migrations/20261004_case_view_read_grants.sql` grants the service role `SELECT` (only) on `sales.call_charge` and `sales.call_held_evidence` for the dashboard's direct reads.
- Changing a customer's plan (platform admins): `POST /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/subscription/switch` with `target` (`standard | growth | pay_per_call | managed`), a caller-chosen `switch_id` (resending it resumes the same switch) and `preview: true` for a dry run (steps, seats, open invoices, the old plan's final-invoice credit, warnings). In one operation it creates the new subscription (or a checkout link when there is no card, `outcome: awaiting_payment`; the old subscription is canceled when the link is paid), points the row at it with `billing_model` / `billing_model_since`, optionally voids the old plan's open invoices (`void_open_invoices`) and cancels the old subscription now with proration. Seats and access never change during the switch. Logic in `services/plan_switch.py`. `POST .../subscription/cancel-now` (`stripe_subscription_id?`, `prorate`, `void_open_invoices`) cancels the linked subscription, or another live one of the same Stripe customer, immediately. `POST .../subscription/create` takes `replace_existing` and `cancel_replaced`, `POST .../subscription/link` takes `cancel_replaced`, and `GET .../subscription` lists `other_subscriptions` (live Stripe subscriptions the row does not point at). A trial payment link refuses (409) a customer with a live subscription unless `replace=true`. Managed engagements paid outside Stripe record installments: `POST /api/v1/admin/managed/engagements/{id}/payments`, `PUT .../installments` (`due_cents`, `next_installment_at`), `DELETE .../payments/{payment_id}`; `payment_status` becomes `partially_paid` until `paid_cents` reaches `due_cents` (table `sales.managed_engagement_payment`, migration 20261002d).
- New customer shortlist (`sales.shortlist`, migration 20261006_customer_shortlist): after onboarding, an admin builds 15-20 companies with the person to write to, the first message, why, and sources, sends it, and the customer answers per company (in target or not, with a reason) and per message (ok or change, with a note) before the autopilots start. Admin (`require_admin`): `GET /api/v1/admin/shortlists?includeClosed=`, `GET|PUT /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/shortlist` (the view: shortlist, ICPs, recipients; PUT saves the whole draft and never overwrites customer feedback on the same item id), `GET .../shortlist/candidates?icpId&q&limit&offset` (the ICP's matched companies by score with people and sources, read-only), `POST .../shortlist/draft-message` (one DeepSeek call drafting a message from the seller's prompts and the item's facts; nothing written but the agent run), `POST /api/v1/admin/shortlists/{id}/send` (`recipient_emails?`, `notify`; visible in the app and emailed from `team@sales.thesimplplatform.io`, replies to the admin; sending again re-notifies and keeps the first date), `POST .../close`. Customer (`require_user`): `GET /api/v1/app/shortlist` (`state`: none, preparing (a draft: no items), sent, answered, closed), `PUT /api/v1/app/shortlist/items/{item_id}/feedback`, `POST /api/v1/app/shortlist/answer` (Discord notice). `sales.my_shortlist_state()` (authenticated, no argument, `auth.uid()`) tells the app's middleware to land the customer on `/shortlist` while one is in progress. Nothing claims leads or reaches outreach.
- Moving an existing customer to pay per call (platform admins): `POST /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/pay-per-call-move` with `switch_id`, `preview`, `icp_prices` (`[{icp_id, cents}]`, `cents: null` = the calculator's price; ICPs left out keep theirs), `default_call_price_cents` (left out: unchanged), `move_to_v2` (default true), `prorate`, `void_open_invoices`, `recipient_email`. In order: switching a switched-off customer back on (`switch_on`, default true: `metadata.is_active`, its ICPs via `activate_customer_cascade`, and `enable_user_ids` or, left out, all its disabled people via `enable_user_account`, which lifts the login ban; ICPs that come back are priced by the calculator at once), the call prices (so the first call billed after the switch has them), the plan switch above to `pay_per_call` (skipped when the customer already bills per call), then `sales.user.ui_version = 'v2'` for the customer's active people (the new Home). Returns `outcome` (`preview | completed | awaiting_payment`), `customer_active_before`, `needs_card`, the ICPs' prices before/after, the team's sign-in and Home before/after, the plan switch response and four `steps` (`switch_on`, `prices`, `plan`, `home`). Everything the customer set up (ICPs, voice, team, accounts) stays. `PUT /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/ui-version` (`ui_version: classic | v2`, `user_ids?`) moves people between the classic app and the new Home. A person added to a company without a Home of their own takes the one most of their active teammates have (trigger, migration 20261005_user_ui_version_follows_team). Logic in `services/pay_per_call_move.py`.
- SimpL Growth: seats at an intro price for N months, then a regular price (defaults EUR 200 x 3 months, then EUR 350, per seat per month, VAT on top). Plan defaults: `GET /api/v1/admin/billing/plans`, `PUT /api/v1/admin/billing/plans/growth` (platform admins). Payment links, `POST /admin/customers/{id}/subscription/create` and onboarding `billing_options` take `product = "growth"` with `intro_price_eur`, `intro_months`, `regular_price_eur` (left-out terms take the defaults). A live Standard subscription moves to Growth in place with `POST /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/subscription/growth` (`effective: next_renewal | now`, `dry_run`), its price changes with `POST .../subscription/growth/price`, and `GET /admin/customers/{id}/subscription` returns its state under `growth`. Stripe objects: `scripts/stripe_setup_growth.py`.
- Business numbers (platform admins) come from one engine (`services/billing/metrics.py` for today, `services/billing/history.py` for months, Europe/Rome), with every hover sentence in `services/billing/definitions.py` and returned by the API: MRR = expected monthly billing net of VAT and of discounts still running at the next renewal, of every subscription in force (active, past due, unpaid, canceling until it ends; paused collection in and flagged), plus Managed engagements billed by invoice (seats × monthly seat price) and each pay-per-call customer's charges of the last 90 days ÷ 3; Cash in = paid invoices by payment date, net of VAT, minus refunds; To collect = open invoices, VAT included; churn per organization (a plan change is not churn) by `churnBasis` = `actual_end` (default: the month access ended) or `request` (the month they asked to cancel); SimpL's own account is out of every total. `POST /api/v1/admin/business/kpis` takes `months` or `historyFrom`/`historyTo` (yyyy-MM-dd) and `churnBasis`; each row carries `description`, `sub_label`/`sub_values` (e.g. "without SimpL"), `cell_hints` and `derived`; past months follow invoices (by the period their lines bill), the current month today's subscriptions. `POST /admin/business/revenue` returns `mrr_cents`, `cash_in_cents`, `customers`, `new_customers`, `churned_customers`, `churn_rate` series. `POST /api/v1/admin/overview/kpis` takes `churnBasis` and returns `descriptions` with MRR/ARR, "of which paid for this period", active users (at least one SimpL action: a lead claimed in SimpL, a connection request, LinkedIn message or email sent, a pending action approved; the extension heartbeat is a separate "extension online"), engagement (users of customers with real product access with 5+ SimpL actions in the last 7 business days), new customers (first paid subscription, out-of-band invoice or Managed engagement in the month, month to date against the same days of last month) and churn. Deploy the dashboard with the API: the payload keys changed (`customer_mrr` is gone, the BP actuals read `total_mrr`).
- Plans & prices: `GET /api/v1/admin/billing/plans/overview` (platform admins) returns every plan on sale (`standard`, `growth`, `managed`, `pay_per_call`, plus `other` when a live subscription sits on a price the catalog does not know) with its list prices, its totals (customers, paying customers, billed seats, net MRR, by the Business definitions) and one row per live subscription on it (payment state, seats, seat price, MRR, renewal, discounts, `growth` state, `pay_per_call` model and ledger totals). A pay-per-call row also says what its next invoice carries: `open_calls` / `open_amount_cents` (billable charges not invoiced yet, `held` included, at their call prices), `call_discounts` (the subscription's discounts still running at the renewal) and `open_due_cents` (after them; 0 for a 100% gift, null with no live subscription). The `pay_per_call` plan sums those over the customers its totals count in `calls` (`calls`, `amount_cents`, `due_cents`); other plans have `calls: null`. `growth_defaults` carries the Growth terms on sale. It reads the snapshot `/admin/business/*` reads: refresh it with `POST /admin/business/refresh`. It does NOT change a plan or a price.
- SDR identities (SimpL's own LinkedIn sending accounts): `GET /api/v1/admin/sdr-identities/activity` (platform admins, `?refresh=true` skips the 10-minute cache) returns every user of SimpL's own customer with, per month from the first outreach event to today (Europe/Rome), `invites`, `accepted`, `messages`, `first_messages` (leads whose first successful LinkedIn message fell in the month), `first_messages_replied`, `first_contacts` (first invite or message per lead), `send_days` and `booked` (attributable first bookings from `sales_view.company_funnel_facts`, on the account whose lead moved), plus weekly `invites` / `first_messages`; `is_identity` is false for SimpL people writing from their own profile (`NOT_SDR_IDENTITY_EMAILS`), `partial_months` flags the first and current month. It only counts: what SimpL pays the identities is computed by the dashboard tool `/dashboard/tools/sdr-identities` (`services/sdr_identities.py`).
- ICP cards: `GET /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/icp-snapshot?days=30` (admins, 1-90 Europe/Rome days ending today, `timezone` and `definitions` in the response) returns, per ICP of the customer: `pool_size` (companies matching it today), `proposal_days` / `claim_days` (dense per day: companies proposed to its users, distinct per day; connections created), `proposals` (distinct companies over the window), `claims` (deprecated sum) with `companies_claimed` / `leads_claimed` apart, all-time `meetings`, `calls_held`, `won` (= `closed`, deprecated) from `company_funnel_facts`, SimpL's only (attributable), plus the window's `meetings_in_window` / `calls_held_in_window` / `won_in_window` (a company counts under the ICP it was first claimed from), `customers` (companies in Customer today, never a win), `users` (assigned, with `is_active`, `daily_lead_limit`, `autopilot_enabled`), `setup` (enabled filters, industries and signals, roles, workflow name, verification, cross-ICP exclusion) and `call_pricing` (price, its source, the pool it was priced at, and the billable charges the ledger priced at it). One read for every ICP; `GET /admin/icps/{id}/stats` stays the single ICP's deep view.
- A signal is stored only if simpl_flow's matcher can run it: exactly one condition, either a post signal (below), an article signal (`collection` + `vector_name` + `embedded_text`; the 1024-d query vector is computed on save) or a SQL signal (`table`, `filters` as a list of SQL strings). Anything else (an empty filter, several conditions, an article without text) is a 422 `Invalid signal: ...` on the admin signal routes, the ICP duplicate route and the onboarding drafts `PUT` (which also computes article vectors, since finalize copies drafts as they are).
- ICP post signals (`/api/v1/admin/icps/{id}/signals`, admins) are one search over the LinkedIn posts index, run by simpl_flow: `{"mode": "all_match", "conditions": [{"collection": "sales_posts", "search_mode": "semantic" | "keyword" | "hybrid", "embedded_text": "<what the posts are about>", "similarity_threshold": 0.5, "keywords": ["series A", "seed round"], "keyword_match": "any" | "all", "date_range_days": 30}]}`. `semantic` reads the topic and threshold, `keyword` the keywords (posts containing any of them, or all: whole words, a phrase's words together and in order, case and accents ignored, no stemming), `hybrid` both (on topic and holding the keywords). Without `search_mode`, keywords plus a topic are `hybrid`, keywords alone `keyword`, else `semantic`. Create and update store only the keys of the mode (threshold strictly between 0 and 1, default 0.5; 1-20 keywords of at most 100 characters, a list or a comma-separated string, duplicates dropped; `keyword_match` default `any`; days 1-365, default 30) and drop the rest, `min_matches` and `embedding_vector` included: one matching post makes a company match, and simpl_flow embeds the text itself. A mode without its text or keywords, a value out of range or a SQL condition on `sales.post` is a 422; `POST .../signals/preview` answers 400 for a post signal (it previews articles only). The onboarding drafts `PUT` applies the same rule. Matches point at `sales.post` ids (`payload.matching_objects["sales.post"]`).
- ICP website signals search the companies' own sites (the landing index, run by simpl_flow) the same three ways: `{"mode": "all_match", "conditions": [{"collection": "landing_pages", "search_mode": "semantic" | "keyword" | "hybrid", "embedded_text": "<what the pages say>", "similarity_threshold": 0.5, "keywords": ["ISO 9001"], "keyword_match": "any" | "all"}]}`. Same rules as post signals (`services/search_signals.py`), but no `date_range_days`: pages carry no date. Matches point at page URLs and carry what to show: `payload.matching_objects["landing_pages"] = {"ids": [<url>], "evidence": [{"url", "title", "section", "page_kind", "snippet"}]}` (the export and the customer agent read the evidence; there is no table to look up). `POST .../signals/preview` answers 400 for website signals too.
- Calls with leads (`/api/v1/app/calls`): `POST /confirm` records whether a call took place (`{company_id | ulc_id, outcome: held | no_show | rescheduled, held_at}`); held moves the owner's leads at the company into the workflow's Negotiation stage, dated by the call (409 when the workflow has none). `GET /pending-confirmation` lists the caller's companies in Meeting Booked; `GET /billing-status` says, for a pay-per-call customer, whether each company's call is billable, why not, and where its charge stands. When booked meetings are: `GET /meetings` gives each company's booking of the caller's customer (`meeting_at`, `to_confirm`, `status`, `outcome_due` two days after it with nothing recorded); `POST /meetings` `{company_id, meeting_at? | capture_job_id?}` records the date, a calendar event, or neither (not fixed yet) and never moves the company; `GET /meetings/calendar-events?company_id=` lists the caller's synced calendar events with that company's people. `GET /journey-log/{id}/details` carries `meeting_recap` on a "How did the meeting go?" card.
- LinkedIn voice messages: `POST /api/v1/app/extension/voice-transcripts` (raw audio body, max 3 MiB, container read from the bytes) returns `{transcript}` for the extension, through the simpl_sales proxy at `/api/v1/linkedin_data/voice_transcript`. Auth is `get_current_user` plus `require_extension_access`. Optional `?event_urn=` (the LinkedIn event) and header `x-simpl-extension-version` are only kept on the outcome row. Nothing is retried and the transcript is never stored: 413/415 refuse the audio before any paid call, 502 means the provider failed, 422 an empty transcript, and in every case the extension simply does not send that voice. Each request past the access check leaves one `app.operation_log` row (see Operation log). The cost goes through `simpl_tracker.clients.openai_audio.transcribe_audio` (`gpt-4o-mini-transcribe`, process `linkedin_voice_transcription`): tracker transport accounting does not see transcriptions, so do not call `audio.transcriptions.create` directly.
- Operation log (`app.operation_log`, `services/operation_log.py`): one row per outcome of a best-effort operation that can drop something unnoticed (`operation`, `outcome` succeeded/skipped/failed, `reason` code, `subject`, `source`, `client_version`, small flat `details`). simpl_api endpoints write their own rows with `record_operation` (source `simpl_api`); the extension reports what the backend never sees, e.g. a voice it could not download, through `POST /api/v1/app/extension/operation-logs` (source `extension`, `get_current_user` plus `require_extension_access`, 204), behind the simpl_sales proxy `/api/v1/extension/operation-logs`. Allowed operations are the `Operation` literal in `services/operation_log.py` (today `linkedin_voice_transcription`): a new use case adds its name there, no migration. Writing a row never raises and never fails the operation it describes; nothing reads the rows to decide anything. Migration `migrations/20261002_operation_log.sql` (backend-only, RLS on).
- Meetings and calls are counted once, the same everywhere: `sales_view.company_funnel_facts(customer_ids, since)` (a company's first booking, its first call that took place, its first close, and whether SimpL brought it).
- Status history and dispatch (2026-10-04): a history row can carry `change_ref` (`journey_log:<uuid>`, `pipeline_run:<id>`, `review:<journey uuid>`, `crm_sync:<id>`, `import:<id>`), set with the transaction setting `sales.status_change_ref` beside `sales.status_changed_by` / `status_change_reason` / `status_effective_at`. A client that cannot set transaction settings (PostgREST) moves a connection with `sales.apply_status_change(p_ulc_id, p_status_id, p_changed_by, p_reason, p_change_ref)` -> boolean (false when unchanged or not the caller's under RLS; an `authenticated` caller may only pass `user:<its id>` or `agent_suggestion`). `sales.user_dispatch_blockers(p_user_id, p_at)` (service_role) lists why the extension hands a seller no task: `code` (`automation_off`, `vacation`, `outside_active_hours`, `extension_offline`, `daily_limit_reached`), Italian `label`, `detail`, `until`. A review action keeps the customer's answer in `payload.review_response`.
- Metrics contract (one definition per number, 2026-10-02; definitions and hover texts in simpl_core `services/reports/definitions.py`, full API in `audits/2026-10-02-metrics-check/API_CONTRACT_PHASE3.md`): every metrics endpoint takes `from` / `to` as calendar days in `timezone` (default Europe/Rome), `includeExternal` (default false: only leads and companies SimpL brought) and `view` (`all` default / `cohort`: the headline of split numbers), and returns `definitions`. The numbers per customer come from `sales_view.customers_metrics_overview_v2` (migration `20261002f`): companies and people taken apart (`companies_claimed`, `leads_claimed`), distinct companies proposed, `leads_contacted` / `leads_replied` / `companies_engaged` / `meetings` / `calls_held` / `won` each as `{value, total, cohort, earlier}`, `meetings_other` / `calls_held_other` (not SimpL's), rates on the cohort (`response_rate`, `booking_rate`, `show_rate`, `win_rate`: `{value, numerator, denominator}`, value null without a denominator), `active_users` (at least one SimpL action, never the extension heartbeat), `pipeline` / `customers` / `won_all_time` (stock). `POST /api/v1/admin/reports/customers-metrics-overview` returns them per customer plus `totals` and `totalsWithoutSimpl` (keeps the old `outreach` / `opportunities` / `trends` columns, deprecated); `POST /admin/reports/customer-metrics` and `POST /api/v1/app/reports/customer-metrics` one customer's (app: `scope: user` for the user's own). `POST /api/v1/app/sales-metrics/metrics` takes `includeExternal` / `view` and returns the contract in `comparison.current.contract` and `definitions`; its old fields now carry the contract (`meetingsBookedInPeriod` = SimpL's meetings, `responseRate` = cohort, `won` = won with SimpL all time, `wonInPeriod`, `customers`, `companiesClaimed` / `leadsClaimed`). `meetingBooked` / `closed` there are where companies stand today, whatever the period. The customer page summaries and Sankeys take the same flags: outreach counts people only, the opportunities Sankey adds `companyFunnel` (proposed -> taken -> contacted -> engaged -> meeting -> call -> won, companies only) and counts proposals as distinct companies; Report Wrapped months are Europe/Rome months with `won` = companies won in the month and an average without SimpL and never-billed accounts.

## Common pitfalls

- Do not duplicate shared logic already available in `simpl_core`.
- Do not add undocumented environment variables.
- Do not run real external provider calls in tests unless explicitly marked/configured.
- Do not give a challenge endpoint a product guard, or a product endpoint `require_challenge_participant`. The two contexts are separate types precisely so the substitution does not typecheck.
- Do not send a price, promotion code or return URL to the payer's `POST /api/v1/onboarding/session/{id}/checkout-intent`: `price_override`, `promo_override`, `success_url` and `cancel_url` are ignored and logged. An admin sets a custom price or code in the setup invite's `billing_options`, and Stripe always returns to `ONBOARDING_PUBLIC_BASE_URL/onboarding?checkout=success|cancel`. `.../subscription/confirm` accepts only the checkout that session's checkout-intent created.
- Onboarding helpers for the invited flow: `POST /api/v1/onboarding/session/{id}/website-brief` reads the session's company website (Firecrawl/Jina scrape plus the first-run quick-context turn, traced as `first_run_quick_context`) and returns editable answers (`status: ok | unreadable | no_source`, `value_proposition`, `summary`, `target_roles`, `target_geographies`, `company_size_min/max`); nothing is saved. `GET|POST /api/v1/onboarding/session/{id}/team-invites` (`{invites: [{email, role}]}` up to 20; role `sales_user` (sending account), `manager` (admin) or `billing_admin` (billing manager, invited with `requires_payment=false`: the owner still pays)) lets the person setting up a new customer (invite role `owner`, `manager` or `billing_admin`) invite teammates on the session's customer setup before the customer exists; each address succeeds or fails on its own (`{sent, failed}`), and a session without a pending/ready setup gets 409. The owner can hand the card to a billing manager: `POST /api/v1/onboarding/session/{id}/payment/delegate {email}` invites (or upgrades) a paying `billing_admin` with the owner's billing options and closes the owner's card step; `POST .../payment/delegate/cancel` takes it back until a card is connected. The snapshot exposes `context.payment_delegation`. Checkout-intent refuses a session that does not pay and any setup that already has a card.

## Operational alerts

- `simpl_api.startup.agent_framework_failed` posts to Discord #error when the agent framework wiring fails at startup. The deploy does not come up.
- Agent framework failures reach #warning once. Chat alerts carry `user_id`, `customer_id`, `conversation_id` and `turn_id`; challenge alerts carry `participant_id`, `contest_id`, `scenario_id` and `turn_id`; Sales Domain wake alerts carry `user_id`.
- `agent_framework.engine.validator_failed` in `pipeline_ops_agent` is promoted to #error. The rule is registered additively, so another app's rules registered in the same process stay in place.
- Requires simpl_tracker `f581b40` or later (notify API version 3, report-once through `raise ... from`, the `__simpl_notify__` silent marker) and simpl_core `855253e` or later (`add_alert_rules`, `labels` on the Sales Domain wrappers).

## Testing

No work in `simpl_api` should be considered complete until the full repo test suite has been run successfully. Targeted tests help during iteration, but they do not replace the final full-repo run. [per simpl-testing-policy]

```bash
conda activate simpl_api
poetry install
poetry run pytest
poetry run ruff check .
```

## What this service does NOT do

- Does not own reusable platform domain primitives. Put those in `simpl_core`.
- Does not own database ORM primitives. Use `simpl_orm`.
- Does not schedule long-running Prefect workflows. Use `simpl_flow`.
- Does not own frontend UI. Use `simpl_sales` or `simpl_dashboard`.

